  "blocks": [
    [
      445.4345703125,
      31.672800064086914,
      474.5144958496094,
      40.0312385559082,
      "Test 2\n",
      0,
      0
    ],
    [
      43.76603317260742,
      66.22492980957031,
      449.7392272949219,
      130.73086547851562,
      "This increasing ability to measure factors related to weather helped scientists to understand\nthe atmosphere and its processes better, and they started collecting weather observation\ndata systematically. In the eighteenth century, the scientist and politician Benjamin Franklin\ncarried out work on electricity and lightning in particular, but he was also very interested in\nweather and studied it throughout most of his life.\nt was Franklin who discovered that storms\nQ39\ngeneraily travel from west to east.\n",
      1,
      0
    ],
    [
      44.05396270751953,
      139.3603057861328,
      449.9234313964844,
      182.27114868164062,
      "In addition to new meteorological instruments, other developments contributed to our\nunderstanding of the atmosphere. People in different locations began to keep records, and in\nthe mid-nineteenth century, the invention of the telegraph made it possible for these records\n@40\nto be collated. This led, by the end of the nineteenth century, to the first weather services.\n",
      2,
      0
    ],
    [
      44.629852294921875,
      190.32469177246094,
      402.4747009277344,
      209.912841796875,
      "It was not until the early twentieth century that mathematics and physics became part of\nmeteorology, and we'll continue from that point next week.\n",
      3,
      0
    ],
    [
      461.2709655761719,
      665.41650390625,
      477.3549499511719,
      673.4866943359375,
      "107\n",
      4,
      0
    ]
  ]
